A

1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 a6 4.Ba4 Be7 5.O-O Nf6 6.Re1 b5 7.Bb3 O-O 8.c3 Na5 9.Bc2 d5 is the normal Ruy Lopez set-up. 10.d4 dxe4 11.Nxe5 c5 12.d5 Bd6 13.Ng4 Nxg4 14.f3 exf3 clears the c2–h7 diagonal.

15.Bxh7+ Kxh7 is the c1-bishop c2–h7, now legal because e4 is empty after 14…exf3. 16.Qxf3 follows. 16…Qh4 is now legal because 12…Bd6 has vacated e7 and 13…Nxg4 has vacated f6, so d8–e7–f6–g5–h4 is open. 17…Qxe1+ is a real e-file check on Kg1, 18.Qf1 interposes on f1 and 19.Qxe1 is forced.

From there the piece trail is coherent:

* 18…Nc4 is a5–c4, 19…Nge5 is g4–e5, 20.Bf4 is Bf1–f4;
* 20…Nd2 is c4–d2 and 21.Nxd2 is Nb1–d2;
* 21…Nf3+ is e5–f3+ on Kg1, 22.Kf2 escapes it and 22…Nxe1 is f3–e1;
* 23.Rxe1 is Rg1–e1, 24.Bxd6 is Bf4–d6, 24…Rxe1, 25.Kxe1 etc.

The rest 26.Bxc5 Bxd5 27.Kf2 Bxa2 28.b4 Rf7 29.Bxf8 Bd5 30.Nf3 Kg6 is a normal liquidation of the h7-sacrifice with all moves geometrically possible, no castling out of check, no illegal captures and a consistent bishop / knight / rook trail.

The other lines all break on elementary legality, e.g. B has 16.Nc4 followed by 18.c4 which tries to play a pawn onto c4 occupied by White’s own knight, and the c4-pawn / knight conflict is fatal. C, D and E all contain impossible captures / castling / checks on close inspection, whereas the Ruy Lopez Bxh7 line in A can be played to move 30 without a rules violation.

