PRIVATE REFLECTION: The deliberation has converged strongly on A as the only move-by-move legal presentation, with 15+ colleagues now citing the Ruy Lopez Bxh7 sacrifice and the two-bishop distinction as the decisive argument. The core A argument that is repeated across the round is:

* the f1-bishop trail 3 Bb5 4 Ba4 7 Bb3 9 Bc2 → 15 Bxh7+ Kxh7 16 Qxf3 is geometrically real once 10 dxe4 and 14 exf3 vacate e4 on c2-d3-e4-f5-g6-h7;
* 12 Bd6 vacates e7 and 13 Nxg4 vacates f6 so d8-e7-f6-g5-h4 is open for 16 Qh4 and 17 Qxe1+ is a real e-file check, with 18 Qf1 interposing;
* the c1-bishop is distinct and plays 20 Bf4 c1-d2-e3-f4 → 24 Bxd6 → 26 Bxc5 → 29 Bxf8, while the f1-bishop is lost on h7;
* 21 Nxd2 is Nb1-d2, 21 Nf3+ is Ne5-f3+, 23 Rxe1 is Ra1-e1, 25 Kxe1 etc. are coherent, no castling through/out of check, no king walks through check.

The B camp is now down to Colleague 3 alone in round 2. Colleague 3 claims B can be written without rule/geometry violation, with 13 Qe4 vacating d4 for 13 Bxb2, 14 Bxa1 15 Rxa1 Rf1-a1, 16 Nc4 17 Ne5 vacating c4 so 18 c4 is a legal c2-c4 pawn push, 18 Nc3 d5-c3, 19 Nxe2+ c3-e2+ on Kg1, and 30 d3+ d4-d3 on Ke2. The A camp’s counter-arguments that persist are:

* 11 Nxd4 Nxd4 cannot be made by a knight from d5 to d4 after 9 exd5 Nxd5; the notation forces an ambiguous “which N” choice;
* 16 Nc4 puts a white knight on c4 and 18 c4 is a pawn move onto an occupied square, and the knight trail Nc4-Ne5-Nc3 is inconsistent with the earlier d5/c6 knight placement;
* the first-rank a1 exchange 13 Bxb2 14 Bxa1 15 Rxa1 is blocked by the queen on d4 / the c1-bishop line and cannot be realised with the pieces that remain after the d4 exchanges;
* 21 Qb2 is blocked on e2-d3-c2-b2 by the White Nd2 that was played on 14 Nd2.

C, D and E remain demonstrably illegal in the current round:

* C 29 Rxe4+ is a direct rook check on White king on e1 and 30 O-O is castling out of check; 10 Bg4 is not a bishop move from f6/e7.
* D 5 Kxd1 moves the White king, so 13 Re1 is blocked by the king on d1 and 21 O-O-O is impossible; White has lost all castling rights.
* E 19 Qf5 is blocked on c2-d3-e4-f5 by the Nd3 left on d3 after 16 Nxd3, 28 Rxf3 cannot be made by either rook on c2/c4 after 26 R8c4, and other rook/bishop route inconsistencies.

Unresolved tension remains with Colleague 3’s specific rook/bishop coordination objection to A: that Re8 and Rf7 cannot be realised simultaneously with the c8-bishop staying on c8 until 25 Bb7. The A camp’s trace resolves this as Re8 = f8-e8 and Rf7 = f8-f7 after the e8 rook has been used on e1 and captured on 25 Kxe1, i.e. the rook that moves to e8 is the kingside f8 rook, not a8-e8, and the later Rf7 is the same rook repositioned after the exchange on e1. That explanation has not been directly refuted in the current round.

Overall current evidence still points to A as the intended valid game. B is defensible only under a very permissive reading of ambiguous captures and knight disambiguation, and the 16 Nc4/18 c4 own-piece occupancy and the d5-d4 knight move remain the strongest elementary refutations. C,D,E are conclusively illegal.

